=F0=9F=93=9A FOUNDER PLAYBOOK: Ask your users to change one =
thing. Not two.

A wearable that worked. Zero adoption.Before Wispr Flow =
was software, it was a neural-signal earpiece converting silent speech to =
text. The tech worked. But it demanded two behavior changes at once: wear a=
 new device AND speak instead of type. Humane Pin hit the same wall (new =
device AND palm projector). Google Glass too (face camera AND voice =
commands in public). Two new behaviors. Every time.They stripped it to one =
ask.Wispr Flow became software. One behavior change: speak instead of type.=
 Apps, screen, workflow unchanged. Then the product learned each user. =
Misspelled names got remembered. Tone matched your writing style. "Tomorrow=
, no wait, Friday" became "Let's meet Friday." 50% month-over-month user =
growth. 90% word-of-mouth.500 onboarding calls showed them what to subtract=
.Tanay Kothari onboarded the first 500 users himself on Google Meet, =
watching where they got confused in real time. The winning features =
subtracted friction. Personal dictionary (stop re-spelling names). Tone =
settings (stop re-formatting). Self-correction (one take, clean copy). =
Three features, three fewer asks.

=F0=9F=8E=AF STEAL THIS:
The behavior-change audit. Before your next launch:1. List every new =
behavior your product requires.2. Circle the one that delivers core value.3=
. Everything else: can you plug into what the user already does?Tanay =
Kothari's breakdown

